        id: "pubintl-treaty-formation-interpretation",
        title: "The Law of Treaties: Formation, Interpretation, and Reservations",
        explanation:
          "The Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties, the VCLT [1], codifies much of this field and is itself widely treated as reflecting customary international law even for non-parties. Formation runs through negotiation, adoption, and authentication of the text, followed by a state's expression of consent to be bound, most commonly through ratification, acceptance, approval, or accession, at which point the treaty enters into force according to its own terms — often requiring a minimum number of ratifications. Once in force, pacta sunt servanda, VCLT Article 26, binds parties to perform the treaty in good faith, and Article 27 bars a state from invoking its own domestic law as an excuse for non-performance. Interpretation follows a structured sequence under Articles 31-32: start with the ordinary meaning of the treaty's terms in their context and in light of the treaty's object and purpose (the 'general rule'), and resort to supplementary means — the treaty's drafting history (travaux préparatoires) and the circumstances of its conclusion — only to confirm that reading or where the general rule leaves the meaning genuinely ambiguous or leads to a manifestly absurd result. A reservation, a unilateral statement purporting to exclude or modify the legal effect of specific provisions as applied to the reserving state, is governed by Articles 19-23: permitted unless the treaty forbids reservations generally or of that specific type, or the reservation is incompatible with the treaty's object and purpose.",
