        explanation:
          "The traditional, widely cited criteria for statehood come from Article 1 of the 1933 Montevideo Convention: a permanent population, a defined territory, an effective government, and the capacity to enter into relations with other states. These criteria are about factual control and organization, not legitimacy or legality of origin, which is why an entity can satisfy them even where its creation is contested. Recognition by other states is governed by two competing theories: the declaratory theory, now the dominant view, treats recognition as merely acknowledging a status (statehood) that already exists as a matter of fact once the criteria are met; the constitutive theory treats recognition itself as what creates statehood, meaning an entity meeting every factual criterion remains, on this view, not fully a state until enough other states recognize it. In practice, most international lawyers and most state practice favor the declaratory view, though recognition still carries enormous practical consequences — treaty-making capacity, standing before certain courts, diplomatic relations — regardless of which theory one adopts in the abstract. Self-determination, a recognized right under the UN Charter and widely treated as having attained customary (and for some scholars, jus cogens) status in the colonial context, complicates the picture further: it supports a people's claim to independence in some circumstances but does not automatically translate into a unilateral right to secede from an existing state outside the classic decolonization context.",
