          "A state's jurisdiction to prescribe and enforce law beyond pure domestic application rests on several recognized bases: territorial jurisdiction (conduct within the state's own territory, the default and least contested basis); nationality jurisdiction (over a state's own nationals regardless of where they act); the protective principle (conduct abroad threatening the state's own essential security interests); and, far more narrowly, universal jurisdiction over a small category of grave international crimes — piracy historically, and more recently torture, genocide, and certain war crimes — regardless of any territorial or nationality link, justified by the gravity of the conduct and the international community's shared interest in its suppression. Even where a jurisdictional basis exists, immunity can bar actually exercising it. State (sovereign) immunity, under the now-dominant restrictive theory, distinguishes sovereign acts, acta jure imperii, which remain immune from another state's domestic courts, from commercial or private-law acts, acta jure gestionis, which do not. Immunity for individual officials operates on a separate, parallel track: immunity ratione personae attaches to a narrow category of incumbent high officials (heads of state and government, foreign ministers) and covers all conduct, official and private, but ends when the person leaves office; immunity ratione materiae attaches to official acts specifically, can be invoked by any official, and survives leaving office, though whether it survives for grave international crimes remains genuinely contested.",
