        explanation:
          "UNCLOS, the UN Convention on the Law of the Sea [3], organizes the ocean into zones radiating out from a coastal state's baseline, each carrying a different, progressively weaker bundle of coastal-state rights. Internal waters and the territorial sea, up to twelve nautical miles, are subject to full coastal-state sovereignty, subject to a right of innocent passage for foreign vessels through the territorial sea. The contiguous zone, out to twenty-four nautical miles, allows limited enforcement jurisdiction over customs, fiscal, immigration, and sanitary matters. The exclusive economic zone, out to two hundred nautical miles, gives the coastal state sovereign rights over living and non-living natural resources and jurisdiction over specific matters like marine research and environmental protection, but not full sovereignty — other states retain high-seas freedoms like navigation and overflight within it. The continental shelf extends the coastal state's rights over the seabed and subsoil (though not the water column above it) out to two hundred nautical miles or further under specific geological criteria. Beyond all of this lies the high seas, open to all states on a basis of freedom of navigation, fishing, and scientific research, subject to specific treaty-based conservation and environmental obligations. Where two states' coastlines are close enough that their EEZ or continental shelf claims overlap, UNCLOS Articles 74 and 83 require delimitation by agreement to achieve an equitable solution, with the equidistance/relevant-circumstances method now the dominant methodology used by courts and tribunals.",
